# Localization (ALWAYS)

**No raw string literals in emitted output.** Every string a process
emits — to a UI, an HTTP response, a log line, a metric description,
an exception payload — flows through a localization catalog.

## The rule

Every emitted string lives in a catalog and is looked up by key. Code
never carries the literal text. There are **two locales**, picked by
who will see the string:

| Locale | Catalog file | For strings that |
|---|---|---|
| **User locale** | `messages/en-US.edn` (plus future `fr-FR.edn`, `ja-JP.edn`, …) | A user will see in a UI, dashboard, formatted error response, or any other product surface |
| **System locale** | `messages/system.edn` | A user will never see — logs, metric / span descriptions, internal error tags, debug prose, telemetry attributes, exception data shipped to observability pipelines |

Both catalogs live under `resources/config/<component>/messages/`.
Both are looked up via `(msg/t :key)`. The split is about **destination**,
not about whether to localize.

## Why two locales (and not one + a "no" exemption)

The previous version of this rule exempted developer-facing strings
from any catalog. That was wrong for three reasons:

1. **Centralization.** Even developer-facing prose benefits from one
   place to audit, grep, and edit. A dev tightening a log message
   shouldn't have to hunt every emit site.
2. **Sanitization gate.** The catalog is the chokepoint. When a future
   scanner asks "does any emitted string contain PII / secrets /
   internal identifiers," the catalog is the surface to check —
   raw-string-literals scattered through code defeat that.
3. **Reclassification without code change.** If a string we labeled
   "developer-facing" turns out to land on a customer console (a
   support-mode metric panel, a debug dialog), we move the key from
   `system.edn` to `en-US.edn` without chasing emit sites.

## Examples

User-facing — UI labels, displayed errors, body copy:

```clojure
;; BAD — raw string in view
[:h3 "No Workflows Yet"]
[:button "Pause"]

;; GOOD — user-locale lookup
[:h3 (msg/t :workflow/none-heading)]
[:button (msg/t :cp/btn-pause)]
```

Developer-facing — log lines, metric descriptions, anomaly text bound
for observability pipelines:

```clojure
;; BAD — raw string in metric definition
(otel/counter "fleet.events.share"
              "Events appended to the durable event log."
              "1")

;; GOOD — system-locale lookup
(otel/counter "fleet.events.share"
              (msg/t :metric-desc/fleet-events-share)
              "1")
```

```clojure
;; BAD — raw string in log call
(log/info "share-event! conflict on" event-id)

;; GOOD — system-locale lookup
(log/info (msg/t :log/share-event-conflict) {:event/id event-id})
```

```clojure
;; BAD — raw string in anomaly message
(anomaly/anomaly :conflict
                 "share-event!: event-id reuse with conflicting payload-digest"
                 {:event/id id})

;; GOOD — system-locale lookup (anomaly text is observability-bound,
;; not displayed to a user)
(anomaly/anomaly :conflict
                 (msg/t :anomaly/event-conflicting-digest)
                 {:event/id id})
```

If the same anomaly is also rendered into a user-facing error toast,
the UI layer looks up the user-locale equivalent (`(t :error/...)`)
via the explicit error-key the producer attached — never reuses the
system string. See "Anomaly → user-locale mapping" below for the
mechanism.

## Anomaly → user-locale mapping

`:anomaly/type` is a coarse category vocabulary (`:not-found`,
`:conflict`, `:invalid-input`, etc. — see
`foundations/exceptions-as-data`). It can't uniquely identify a
specific user-facing message: the same `:conflict` type covers
"event-id reuse with conflicting digest", "approval already
finalised", "schedule overlap", etc. Mapping must use a finer key.

**Producer responsibility.** When an anomaly might surface in a UI,
the producer attaches an explicit `:user/error-key` keyword to
`:anomaly/data`:

```clojure
(anomaly/anomaly :conflict
                 (msg/t :anomaly/event-conflicting-digest)   ;; system text
                 {:user/error-key :error/event-conflicting-digest
                  :event/id id})
```

**Renderer responsibility.** The UI layer reads
`:user/error-key` and looks it up:

```clojure
(if-let [k (get-in anomaly [:anomaly/data :user/error-key])]
  (msg/t k)
  ;; No producer-attached key → either this anomaly was never meant
  ;; for a user surface, or the producer has a gap. Render a
  ;; generic fallback by :anomaly/type, never the raw system text.
  (msg/t (default-error-key (:anomaly/type anomaly))))
```

The shared `<feature>-<condition>` naming convention from "Key
naming conventions" makes the pairing mechanical: the producer pairs
`:anomaly/event-conflicting-digest` (system) with
`:error/event-conflicting-digest` (user) automatically by reading
its own emit site.

When an anomaly is purely operational (will never reach a UI —
internal sync failures, telemetry-only conditions), the producer
omits `:user/error-key`. Callers that DO render to a UI hit the
fallback path, which surfaces the type-level message rather than
internal text.

## Catalog files are the canonical source

```edn
;; resources/config/<component>/messages/en-US.edn
{:knowledge-shared/messages
 {:workflow/none-heading "No Workflows Yet"
  :workflow/none-body    "Workflows will appear here when you run a workflow"
  :duration/ms-suffix    "ms"}}
```

```edn
;; resources/config/<component>/messages/system.edn
{:knowledge-shared/system
 {:log/share-event-conflict                  "share-event! conflict on event-id"
  :anomaly/event-conflicting-digest          "share-event!: event-id reuse with conflicting payload-digest"
  :metric-desc/fleet-events-share            "Events appended to the durable event log."}}
```

New strings go into the appropriate catalog FIRST, then are
referenced by key.

## Key naming conventions

- Namespace by audience + feature:
  - User locale: `:workflow/`, `:cp/`, `:metric/`, `:error/` (errors
    rendered to the user)
  - System locale: `:log/`, `:anomaly/`, `:metric-desc/`, `:debug/`,
    `:internal-error/`
- Use domain semantics, not UI position: `:workflow/none-heading` not
  `:sidebar/empty-title`
- Suffixes: `-heading`, `-body`, `-hint`, `-label`, `-prefix`,
  `-suffix`, `-placeholder`
- The user-locale `:error/<feature>-<condition>` and system-locale
  `:anomaly/<feature>-<condition>` SHOULD share the trailing pair so
  callers can map between them: `:error/agent-not-found` ↔
  `:anomaly/agent-not-found`.

## What goes where

| Category | Example | Catalog |
|----------|---------|---------|
| UI labels | "Pause", "Submit", "Cancel" | User locale |
| Headings and body copy | "No agents registered" | User locale |
| Error messages displayed to user | "Agent not found" | User locale |
| Units and suffixes shown in UI | "ms", "s", " tokens" | User locale |
| Placeholder / hint text | "Type your response..." | User locale |
| Log messages (developer-facing) | "Error reading file" | System locale |
| Metric / span descriptions (OpenTelemetry, Prometheus, etc.) | "Events appended to the log." | System locale |
| Anomaly `:anomaly/message` text | "share-event!: id reuse with conflicting digest" | System locale |
| Internal error tags shipped in `ex-data` / log fields | "eql-processing-failed" | System locale |
| Code comments | | Not emitted — raw |
| Test assertions | | Not emitted — raw |
| EDN / config keys | `:auto-apply/tick-ms` | Not emitted — raw |
| Source-code identifiers | function / variable names | Not emitted — raw |

The dividing line is **emission**: if the string ends up in a UI, an
HTTP body, a log file, a metric backend, or any other surface a human
or downstream system reads — it goes through a catalog. If it's a
source artifact (comment, identifier, config key, test assertion), it
stays raw.

## Agent behavior

When writing or reviewing code:
1. Every string literal in code that gets emitted is a violation —
   replace with `(msg/t :key)`.
2. When adding a new emitted string, decide audience first:
   - Will any user (operator dashboard counts) see it? → user locale
   - Logs / metric descriptions / observability-bound anomaly text /
     internal tags? → system locale
3. Add the key to the matching catalog file FIRST, then reference it.
4. When reviewing a PR, grep emit sites for raw string literals.
   Each one is a missing catalog key. Patterns to scan:
   - `(log/(info|warn|error|debug|trace) "` — log calls
   - `(otel/counter "` / `(otel/histogram "` / `(otel/gauge "` —
     metric-instrument descriptions (third arg)
   - `(anomaly/anomaly :[^ ]+ "` — anomaly messages
   - `(throw (ex-info "` — surviving ex-info text in boundary
     namespaces (per `foundations/exceptions-as-data`)
   - `[:p "` / `[:h1 "` / `[:button "` — view namespaces
5. Format strings that combine a label + value use separate keys:
   `(str (msg/t :label) value)` not `(str "Label: " value)`.
6. When in doubt about audience, default to **system locale** —
   relocating to user locale later is a one-line catalog move; the
   reverse direction usually means a string a user already saw is
   getting rephrased in a way that reads as a regression.

## Anti-patterns to reject

- Inline strings in Hiccup / view code: `[:p "Loading..."]` →
  user-locale lookup
- Inline strings in `log/` calls: `(log/info "Error reading file")` →
  system-locale lookup
- Inline strings in metric descriptions:

  ```clojure
  ;; BAD
  (otel/counter "x" "Counts things." "1")
  ;; GOOD
  (otel/counter "x" (msg/t :metric-desc/x) "1")
  ```

- Inline strings in anomaly messages:
  `(anomaly/anomaly :type "..." {})` → system-locale lookup
- "Developer-facing strings don't need l10n" — that exemption is
  removed. Developer-facing strings go to the system locale.
- Format strings with embedded units: `(format "%.1fs" n)` — extract
  the `s` suffix to a key
- Duplicate strings across files — one key, referenced in multiple
  places
- Catalogs referencing each other (EDN aliasing, runtime key chain).
  Catalogs are flat; each is the canonical source for its locale.
  When the same condition needs both a system-locale log line AND a
  user-locale toast, define a key in EACH catalog and let the
  rendering layer map between them at render time. The shared
  `<feature>-<condition>` naming convention (see "Anomaly →
  user-locale mapping") makes the pairing mechanical without making
  one catalog depend on the other.
